      \"setting_or_time\": \"week 5-8 of dietary intervention\",\n      \"groups\": [\n        {\n          \"group\": \"high fiber\",\n          \"value\": \"n=11\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"group\": \"control\",\n          \"value\": \"n=4\"\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"research_object\": \"high fiber arm subgroup by ICB regimen\",\n      \"result_or_measure\": \"immune changes more prominent in combination vs monotherapy\",\n      \"setting_or_time\": \"week 5-8 of dietary intervention\",\n      \"groups\": [\n        {\n          \"group\": \"nivolumab + ipilimumab\",\n          \"value\": \"n=3\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"group\": \"pembrolizumab or nivolumab monotherapy\",\n          \"value\": \"n=7\"\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"research_object\": \"serum bile acids (untargeted metabolomics)\",\n      \"result_or_measure\": \"p<0.05 (reduced in high fiber vs control, Wilcoxon rank sum)\",\n      \"setting_or_time\": \"equilibration vs week 1-4\",\n      \"groups\": [\n        {\n          \"group\": \"high fiber\",\n          \"value\": \"n=6\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"group\": \"control\",\n          \"value\": \"n=8\"\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"research_object\": \"mouse FMT recipients per group\",\n      \"result_or_measure\": \"improved ICB response in post-high fiber stool recipients\",\n      \"setting_or_time\": \"mouse avatar model\",\n      \"groups\": [\n        {\n          \"group\": \"post-high fiber diet recipients\",\n          \"value\": \"n=5\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"group\": \"pre-high fiber diet recipients\",\n          \"value\": \"n=5\"\n        }\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"usable_content\": \"The DIET trial (NCT04645680) has published early results on peripheral immune profiling and serum metabolomics, but the available abstracts do not report specific human gut microbiome taxa compositional changes with formal statistical mediation analysis linking microbiome changes to ICI clinical outcomes (ORR, PFS, OS). Abstract 6743 (2026) reports immune changes in melanoma patients randomized 2:1 to high fiber (30-50 g/d, n=11) vs control diet (20 g/d, n=4): decreased circulating macrophages (p=.018), neutrophils (p=.035), and classical monocytes (p=.0059 by CyTOF) within week 5-8, with increased effector-memory T cells (CD8+ p=.01, CD4+ p=.032). These immune changes were more prominent in combination nivolumab+ipilimumab (n=3) vs monotherapy (n=7) within the high fiber arm. Human-to-mouse FMT showed improved ICB response with post-high fiber stool, suggesting microbiome-dependent benefits, but this is translational evidence rather than direct human microbiome mediation data. Abstract 3275 (2026) reports serum bile acid profiling (paired samples: high fiber n=6, control n=8) showing reduced unconjugated secondary bile acids and secondary-to-primary ratios (p<0.05), attributed to reduced microbiota-mediated biotransformation, but does not list specific gut taxa changes in humans. The 2024 protocol publication explicitly states 'No datasets were generated or analysed during the current study' and describes planned whole-genome shotgun sequencing and mediation analyses as pending. Distinction: human immune and metabolic changes are reported with statistical comparisons, but the explicit human microbiome compositional/functional changes statistically mediating clinical efficacy remain unspecified in these abstracts; microbiome dependence is inferred via mouse FMT rather than demonstrated through human mediation analysis.\",\n  \"decision\": \"external_research\",\n  \"still_missing\": \"Specific human gut microbiome taxa or functional pathway changes from the DIET trial with formal statistical mediation analysis linking these changes to ICI clinical outcomes (ORR, PFS, OS) in the human cohort\",\n  \"read_focus\": \"Full-text publications of the DIET trial (NCT04645680) that may contain 16S/metagenomic sequencing data, microbiome compositional changes, and mediation statistics; search for companion reports beyond the 2026 AACR abstracts\",\n  \"external_ask\": \"Search for full-text DIET trial publications (2024-2026) reporting: (1) human stool microbiome sequencing results (taxa abundance shifts or functional pathways) associated with high fiber intervention, (2) statistical mediation analysis linking microbiome changes to ICI clinical outcomes in the human cohort, or (3) explicit statement that microbiome mediation results are pending/unavailable. Also search for other completed human dietary intervention trials in ICI-treated patients that report microbiome mediation data.\",\n  \"reason\": \"The available materials (2026 AACR abstracts and 2024 protocol) provide immune profiling and metabolomics data from the DIET trial with quantitative comparisons, but none report the specific human gut microbiome compositional/functional changes with statistical mediation analysis required by the success criteria. The protocol explicitly confirms microbiome data is pending. Mouse FMT supports microbiome dependence but does not fulfill the requirement for human mediation analysis. This constitutes partial fulfillment: the trial exists and shows microbiome-dependent effects via translational models, but the core question about human microbiome mediation statistics remains unanswered in these sources.\"\n}\n```",
        "reasoning_content": "The user is asking whether the DIET trial (NCT04645680) or other human dietary intervention trials report specific gut microbiome compositional or functional changes that statistically mediate the observed clinical efficacy improvements in ICI-treated patients.\n\nLet me analyze the materials found:\n\n1. **Abstract 6743 (2026)** - Reports peripheral immune profiling from DIET trial\n   - Human data: immune profiling (PBMC, NanoString, CyTOF)\n   - Shows reduced circulating monocytes, increased T cell activation in high fiber arm\n   - Human-to-mouse FMT studies showed improved ICB response with post-high fiber stool\n   - Does NOT report specific human microbiome taxa changes\n   - Uses mouse FMT to infer microbiome-dependent benefits\n\n2. **Abstract 3275 (2026)** - Reports bile acid metabolic profile from DIET trial\n   - Human data: serum metabolomics and bile acid profiling (n=43 patients, paired samples from high fiber n=6 vs control n=8)\n   - Shows reduced unconjugated secondary bile acids and secondary-to-primary BA ratios in high fiber arm\n   - Attributes changes to reduced gut microbiota-mediated biotransformation\n   - Mouse FMT showed improved ICB response with post-high fiber microbiota\n   - Does NOT report specific gut microbiome taxa compositional changes in humans\n   - Does NOT report formal statistical mediation analysis linking microbiome to clinical outcomes in humans\n\n3. **Protocol publication (2024)** - DIET trial protocol\n   - Explicitly states \"No datasets were generated or analysed during the current study\"\n   - Plans whole-genome shotgun sequencing of stool samples\n   - Plans metabolomic profiling and statistical analyses to explore associations\n   - Confirms microbiome mediation data is pending/unavailable in this report\n\nNow I need to extract quantitative comparisons where available and write usable content that accurately represents what these materials do and don't report.\n\n\n \nFor Abstract 6743, I can identify specific immune cell measurements: circulating macrophages decreased (p=.018), neutrophils decreased (p=.035), and classical monocytes decreased (p=.0059) in the high fiber group versus control during weeks 5-8. The high fiber arm had 11 participants compared to 4 in control. T cell activation showed increased frequency of effector-memory T cells (p=.01 in CD8+, p=.032 in CD4+). Within the high fiber arm, combination therapy recipients (n=3) showed more prominent effects than monotherapy recipients (n=7).\n\nFor Abstract 3275, the analysis included 43 patients total with paired samples from 6 high fiber and 8 control participants during weeks 1-4. Bile acid measurements showed reduced unconjugated secondary bile acids and secondary-to-primary ratios with p<0.05 by Wilcoxon rank sum test. Mouse studies used 5 recipients per group.\n\nI need to determine whether these materials directly answer the question about microbiome mediation of clinical efficacy. The abstracts show immune and metabolic changes but don't establish the causal chain from microbiome composition through immune/metabolic changes to clinical outcomes. The protocol confirms microbiome data collection is planned but not yet analyzed. This suggests I need additional sources that specifically report microbiome composition changes linked to clinical response.",
